you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme campaigners say the  government's credibility as hosts of the  cop 26 summit is at risk  as a new oil field in the north sea  awaits final approval to begin  production we find out how transforming  a moor near manchester into a giant  sponge is helping reduce greenhouse gas  emissions  and can gaming make us greener  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  now today boris johnson and the labour  leader sakir stama have been out  visiting  separate wind farms the prime minister  is hoping to highlight the role of  renewable energy  ahead of the cop 26 climate summit  taking place in glasgow in november  but he's facing criticism for not  matching policy  with rhetoric well just a few hundred  miles from glasgow in the north sea  sits the cambo oil field it's awaiting  final approval to go into production  but critics say giving it the go-ahead  would significantly damage the  government's credibility  just months ahead of cop so here's what  the prime minister had to say after his  visit to the moray east offshore wind  farm  this was a contract that was signed in  was agreed in 2001 and we can't just  tear up  uh contracts as a process to be gone  through but what we need to do  is use this incredible potential of wind  power  and turbines like this haven't been  going up they've only been going up in  the last  four or five years the size that you're  looking at now and they're going to get  even bigger so the potential is  absolutely enormous  well our political correspondent joe  pike is in aberdeen for us so joe  a bit of a green political standoff  going on there certainly  is anna boris johnson is in  aberdeenshire today wanting to focus on  wind power as you say investment in  green technology but at the same time  very difficult  questions being asked about how  committed the government really are to  tackling  climate change especially as you say  because this cambo oil field  is likely to be approved very soon  environmental groups  like oxfam estimate that if it does go  ahead 132 million  tons of co2 will be released now boris  johnson was asked yesterday while he was  in scotland  what he thought of the development he  said then he wasn't aware  today kiyosama in glasgow had a much  clearer position  no i don't think that we should go ahead  with cambo  exploration and oil field we are about  to have cop26 here  in scotland in glasgow which is all  about bringing the world together  to take really hard decisions about how  we take the urgent action that's  necessary on climate change  it is completely inconsistent with that  what we need now is a conversation  about how we transition a dr just  transition  but government sources say this is in  the hands of regulators  initial approval to explore the field  was given  20 years ago and if they were to  say no to final approval it could all  end up  in the courts what this does though show  anna is that government decisions  around the climate around energy have  far more scrutiny than they did  a matter of years ago and that clearly  is due to the upcoming clop  cop climate conference in glasgow this  november  joe thanks very much so boris johnson  and kearstarma out visiting wind farms  there  so let's take a quick look at our data  dashboard to see where electricity in  great britain  is coming from today and as you can see  around 26  is coming from fossil fuels we've got 56  coming from renewable sources that  includes wind power  and then 18 from nuclear  now in today's other climate news  campaigners are calling for the end of  driven grouse shooting in at least  10 percent of britain's national parks  research by rewilding britain  found 842 000 acres of protected land  are taken up by intensely managed grouse  moors causing biodiversity loss  and damaging soil the moorland  association  strongly dispute the findings saying  grouse moore managers are committed  to conservation efforts the densest  air quality sensor network in the world  is being installed in north london  to improve people's health and wellbeing  250 advanced air quality sensors will be  fitted on  lamp posts and buildings across camden  and refresh  60 times more frequently than existing  stations  it's hoped the data could help people  map journeys using roots  that have the least air pollution and a  breeding programme  set up to reintroduce ospreys to england  has seen its 200th  chick fledge the female chick will stay  in rutland until september  when it'll head off on a 3 000 mile  migration journey  to the west coast of africa the rutland  osprey project was set up in 1996  more than 150 years after the birds  became extinct  in england and wales to the united  states now and a significant move from  president biden on electric vehicles  well our u.s correspondent mark stone is  in washington for us  so mark what's this all about then well  this is a pledge by uh president biden  to make  half of all electric vehicles all  vehicle sales in the united states by  2030 to be  electric now that is a a massive  commitment he's signing an executive  order  to try and make this happen it's not  binding though and i think some experts  are saying this isn't quite as ambitious  as perhaps it could be  you know i was driving to the office  this morning not in an electric vehicle  and it was quite clear around me here in  dc that hardly any vehicles are electric  and i was looking  at the statistics and they stand that up  you know two percent of  vehicles um in in the united states are  electric compare that to europe 10  percent  the uk 11 china even  5 of the market share is electric so  this gas  guzzling nation is lagging well behind  and this is an attempt by president  biden  with the buy-in it seems of the big car  manufacturers to change that  mark thanks very much indeed last month  was the second warmest july on record  in europe and the third warmest globally  the data from the copernicus climate  change service  shows heatwave conditions were  especially severe in southeastern europe  at the end of the month  warmer than average conditions extended  eastward across most of  asia and the exceptional warmth over the  western u.s and canada  continued from june and the extreme heat  in europe has led to severe wildfires  you can see more about how parts of  turkey have been devastated by flames  in the weekly climate show that's  available on sky news social channels  our app  and our website now pete  is a climate superhero in the uk alone  peatlands hold  around the same amount of carbon as all  the forests in the uk  france and germany combined but much of  it is damaged or dried out  meaning it emits carbon rather than  storing it  well now conservationists are working on  a solution as our science correspondent  thomas moore reports  on the lancashire hills they've created  a giant sponge  dotted across holcomb moore are more  than 3 000 pools  dug into the peat they should slow the  flow of water that used to flood the  towns below  this is a sphagnum plug nick helped  plant half a million patches of moss  that should thrive in the boggy  conditions it takes many times its own  weight in in in water  um so again it's quite right to say it's  a sponge it's like a giant sponge over  over 100 hectares that we're trying to  create here and this little plant here  that's been  around for millennia we always knew  we're important  but really is relevant to modern day  society because this little plant  can is the key to all the success on  this on this project  in a healthy bog the moss sucks in  carbon from the air as it grows  when it dies it doesn't rot because the  ground is so wet  it becomes peat with the carbon locked  away for thousands of years  but on holcomb moor over grazing by  sheep and 150 years of air pollution  have destroyed the fragile moss blanket  a little bit that's been exposed  during the moorland works and you can  see that this has dried out because  um it's been on the surface and you can  see how crumbly it is  and it just the wind takes it and any  water would just take that away  and degrade it and once you start to get  gullies then  you know it just exacerbates the problem  so once it's all vegetated then the  peat's  protected but this success is barely a  hundred hectares  the peat problem is far bigger peat  covers  three million hectares in the uk and it  stores  three billion tons of carbon that's more  than all the forests of the uk  france and germany put together but it  is so  dry that it's releasing 20 million tons  of carbon every year that's  four percent of the uk's annual  emissions  the government says it will now fund  larger projects elsewhere  we've made a commitment to restore 35  000  hectares of peatland by 2025 well that's  only in four years time  and we've already got the measures in  place to set that in motion  repairing the damage took six months but  the benefits will be felt for  generations to come thomas more sky news  on holcomb moore now a new wave of video  games  are rewarding players for making  eco-friendly decisions  in the hope that actions in virtual  worlds  will help encourage real life changes  too now there are more than 44 million  video gamers in the uk  and the industry has grown to become the  country's most lucrative entertainment  sector  more than video and music combined  globally  2.7 billion people play video games and  that's expected to rise to more than 3  billion  by 2023 it's hoped this huge global  audience could start to make a  difference to climate change  now the un believes that video games can  be used to encourage people  to make greener choices and it's working  with companies to explore how games  could address themes  like reforestation and renewable energy  so can gaming make us greener sky's  victoria seabrook has been to find out  a video game with a message we shouldn't  have gone so deep  subnautica is one of a wave of games  trying to tackle climate change  warning players about threats to the  natural world and rewarding them for  protecting it  our world is in free fall  designers say they want their players to  turn the virtual  into reality we wanted people to feel  inspired by what they did as alba  and actions and change their own habits  um outside of the game ecosystem  and parents have got in touch saying  that their children have been begging  them to go and do bird watching and to  go and  do a beach cleanup gaming is everywhere  it's in our homes and on our phones  one in three people on the planet play  what happened to this place  the industry says it can simulate things  like climate change that can otherwise  be hard to imagine  but whether it can really change us is  unclear while we can confidently say  that there's some research which  indicates that people might change how  they think about sustainability in the  environment while they're playing the  game  we're less clear on how those changes  then translate into the real world  and whether they last in the long term  as for the players themselves  our green storylines are win the more  realistic and the more immersive the  game can be  then the more you are definitely going  to play it i don't think that it's  necessarily impacting the  industry i don't think that it's  necessarily impacting consumers the  smaller things that these games are  talking about with  you know recycling and and properly  disposing of plastics and things  they're things that everyone can do of  course like any industry gaming has its  own carbon footprint to clean up  from energy use to electronic waste it  will have to win these battles too  if it wants to go truly green victoria  seabrook sky news  and if you really want to immerse  yourself in the world of green gaming  then tune into this week's  sky news climate cast where we put the  green nudging theory to the test  it's out tomorrow and you can find it  wherever you normally get your podcast  from  well that's everything from us for today  coming up on tomorrow's show  the tension between protecting farms and  looking after a much-loved native  species  as a row over culling beavers in  scotland plays out in the courts  you can catch that at the same time here  on sky news see you then  you

